    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d692">

                <pb facs="0750" n="740" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg740"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.515/2–1649: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Cabot</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-02-16">February 16,
                        1949</date>.<lb/>[Received February 16—5:30 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>573. UP despatch datelined Hong Kong February 15 published <hi
                        rendition="#italic">China Press</hi> 16th reports arrival Amoy from Canton
                    160 tons Government owned gold, silver bullion. Shipments made in two lots. Ten
                    tons gold shipped aboard Chinese Customs cruiser and 150 tons gold and silver
                    aboard <hi rendition="#italic">Hai Ping</hi>. Item also quotes source as stating
                    even larger amounts understood arrived Shanghai en route Formosa.</p>
                <p>Sent Nanking 386, repeated Department 573, Canton 62.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cabot</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
